  <h1>About DaoismHub</h1>
  <p class="lede">Version 2.0 &middot; Last updated: October 21, 2026 &middot; Applies to daoismhub.com and data.daoismhub.com</p>

  <h2>What this site is</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub is an independent educational reference about Chinese contemplative traditions —
    Daoism, Chan Buddhism and related self-cultivation — covering their classical texts,
    history, concepts, practices, modern research and recorded practitioner experience. It is
    built for readers, students, researchers and practitioners who want a structured, sourced
    entry point to the tradition in English, with the original Chinese where it matters.
  </p>
  <p>
    The project is deliberately narrow. It does not sell anything, teach courses, certify
    teachers, offer retreats, or provide advice. It reads, translates, organises and cites —
    and it tries to be honest about what is known, what is disputed and what is simply
    unknown.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It is not a medical, psychological or therapeutic resource, and nothing here is medical advice. Read the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> before attempting any practice described.</li>
    <li>It is not a teacher or a lineage, and it confers no authority to teach or practise.</li>
    <li>It is not a scholarly edition. Translations are editorial renderings, one documented reading among several; scholarly work should consult the Chinese text and standard critical editions.</li>
    <li>It is not a directory of organisations, teachers or products, and it does not accept payment for inclusion or coverage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Editor and author</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ding.W</strong> — a meditation practitioner who maintains this reference in order
    to share traditional Chinese contemplative theory, and the recorded experience of fellow
    practitioners, with an English-language readership.
  </p>
  <p lang="zh">
    禅修练习者，致力于分享中国传统禅修理论和其他禅修者的经验到世界。
  </p>
  <p>
    The editor is responsible for the site's content: what is published, how it is sourced,
    how translations are made, and how corrections are handled. The site is written and
    edited by a human; no content is published unreviewed, and corrections are made on the
    merits rather than to please a source, a tradition or a commercial interest.
  </p>

  <h2>How content is made</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Sourcing first.</strong> Claims are traced to a primary text, a scholarly
      edition or study, or a named tradition — and the source is cited on the page, with a
      link where one exists.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Evidence classes.</strong> Primary sources, scholarly interpretation,
      practitioner reports and editorial analysis are kept distinct and labelled, so a reader
      can see what kind of claim they are reading. The system is documented in the
      <a href="https://data.daoismhub.com/schemas/evidence-system/">Evidence System</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Translations with the Chinese alongside.</strong> Key terms and passages are
      given in the original, with the reading chosen and the variants noted, rather than a
      translation presented as self-evident.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Disagreement recorded, not smoothed.</strong> Where traditions, editions or
      scholars disagree — on a date, an attribution, a reading or a practice — the page says
      so. Where evidence is thin, the page says that too.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dated and revisable.</strong> Pages carry their update dates and are revised
      when a source changes or an error is found.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Editorial independence and funding</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub carries no advertising, no sponsored content, no affiliate links and no paid
    placements. No tradition, teacher, organisation, publisher or commercial interest has
    editorial control over what is published, and no payment is accepted for coverage,
    ranking, review or inclusion. The site is independently maintained and is not funded by,
    affiliated with, or acting on behalf of any religious body, institution or company.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where the site links to an external resource, the link is a citation of record — not an
    endorsement, and not a commercial arrangement. There are no commercial arrangements of
    that kind on this site.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    Errors happen, and corrections are part of how the site is maintained. If you find a
    factual error, a mistranslation, a broken citation or an attribution you believe is wrong,
    write to us with the page URL and the specific passage. Substantiated corrections are made
    and dated; where a correction changes the substance of a page, the change is noted on the
    page rather than made silently.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>
    The sites run no accounts, no analytics, no advertising and no cookies, and collect no
    personal data through the browser. Email correspondence is used only to answer you. The
    full position, including your rights and how to exercise them, is in the
    <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>

